of the immune component and the tumor  microenvironment  than we typically would see running  standard immunistic chemistry  and in c2 assays which are far lower  throughput and low plex  so now that i've shown you how we are  combining these two technologies  such as our placebo and the nanostring  dsp profiling  to investigate the tumor  microenvironment rare immune cell  populations i can show you how we've  used this  this approach to evaluate responses to  some of these more  interesting immunomodulatory drugs like  checkpoint  inhibitors so this is particularly  exciting  application of these two technologies is  we can now begin to gain  more mechanistic insight into how these  types of drugs may act  and act together in combinations  in patient tumors without having to  expose patients to the typical systemic  administration  and levels of these types of powerful  immune modulating drugs  so in the clinical setting in soft  tissue sarcoma we've begun evaluating  checkpoint inhibitors uh including drugs  like  nivolumab an anti-pd1 antibody drug  and we do in fact see responses that are  specific  to the mechanism of action of a drug  like nevo  including the activation and cytokine  secretion  of immune cells specifically within  the anti-pd1 drug injection sites as you  can see here on the right hand  panel we see some specific responses in  the anti-pd-1  versus a more traditional classical  chemotherapy and soft tissue  sarcoma tribected in  upon you know relieving t cells from  checkpoint we can see their activation  and secretion of cytokines such as  interferon gamma  and when we stain our sample to look  further downstream  at activation of macrophage and  dendritic dendritic cell population  we can see things such as an increased  production of related  chemokines like cxel9 and cxcl10  so traditional immunohistochemistry and  in c2 staining provides us with some  insight into the activation status of  the immune component of the tumor  microenvironment  but it is definitely not the whole  picture  and so we've been looking for ways to  evaluate biomarkers of checkpoint  inhibitors  across cell types within the tumor  microenvironment  and looking for ways to evaluate many  potential  markers of activation within that  population  so naturally this leads us to  asking you know can we focus our  analysis  of sibo samples on specific cell  populations  and figure out what other gene  transcripts are changing in these  populations  as they are exposed to different drug  treatments such as  specifically the t cell component the  rare cell profiling capability of the  geomet system  allows us now to directly answer this  question and so using again a cta panel  we can do in-depth profiling of not only  the t-cell compartment but also the  adjacent tumor cells and really any  cells that are present within the tumor  microenvironment  from this we are now generating  numerous biomarker readouts that will  help us  inform how these cells are responding  and activating  when exposed to drugs like checkpoint  inhibitors  this is a look at some of the data from  the first time we applied rare cell  profiling to  one of our soft tissue sarcoma clinical  samples  and we were happy to see that we did in  fact confirm  what we'd expected to see using a cd3  masking strategy in genomics we showed  that in our samples  we could enrich the gene expression  readout  for not only the cd3 gene transcript  but also for many t cell and  inflammation associated genes  so in the left hand violin plots here  you see that the cd3 masking strategy  works and it works quite well  and we can show that enrichment of the  cd3 transcript  within the cd3 specific mask and not in  the  inverse tumor mask and now taking this  another step further  and looking deeper into the data in the  right hand volcano plot  you can see the cd3 mask samples are  again  highly enriched for t cell specific  transcripts  like granzyme b and cd8 and  in the tumor cell mask we see enrichment  for  transcripts that are typically more  associated with tumor epithelium  and many of the other cell types that  are in the tumor micro environment  so what happens when we apply rare cell  profiling  and now look more broadly across  multiple different drugs  and drug combinations in the same  patient tumor  so you can see here in this slide this  is a breakdown now of the top 100 or so  genes  that are regulated across the different  treatments  within the different compartments of  this patient's tumor  so you can see here in the  green rose the cd3 positive  cell masking in the inverse  cd3 negative cells or any cell that is  not cd3 positive  you'll see that in the yellow rows and  the combined  cd3 and cd3 negative cells that would be  basically all cells within the tumor and  tumor micro environment  you can see that as as a magenta row  in the figure and in the left hand  section of this particular heat map  you will see the top most mostly rate  down regulated genes  across treatments that's shown mostly in  the blue colors  in the middle section you see the genes  that are  more variably regulated by treatment or  cell type  and and then on the right hand section  you'll see the genes that are  strongly upregulated by most treatments  as you can see here in the darker red  colorations  upon closer look at this data now  we see some genes that are highly  regulated specifically  by treatment and also by cell type  so now taking a much closer look  again at this data in the green circle  we see significant upregulation  of granzyme b in the t cell compartment  of the io combination treatment site  which included  aldous leuken and nivolumab and  unexpectedly in the yellow circle we saw  a significant upregulation of ido1  in all compartments in this particular  combination of all this leukemia and  nevo treatment  this is potentially a significant  finding in this patient's tumor  given idea one's role in tumor immune  tolerance  and the potential as an early mechanism  for resistance  so again looking at this a little more  specifically and drilling down  even a little deeper into the data again  and the plot on the left  we we can highlight and see the t cell  specific elevation of granzyme b  at the site of all this leuken and the  voldemab combination  and we see this across multiple levels  of the patient tumor  that we analyzed similarly in the right  hand plot we see  increased expression of ido1 within the  tumor microenvironment across now  multiple tumor levels  and we see this at sites of aldis leuken  at sites of nivolumab  and with the most significant  significant  elevation of ido1 occurring within the i  o  combination site itself  so based on these findings with the  geomics  digital spatial profiling data set that  we  were able to produce we then took this  and validated some of these observations  using some  adjacent tissue sections and orthogonal  assays such as in situ hybridization  for either cd3 and granzyme b  or idea one itself in this slide here  i'll just point out  you can see the increase in the granzim  b  expressing t cells specifically at the  site  of the combination of all this leukemia  and the volamab injection  and the sebanjected tumor  and similarly we were also happy to see  that we confirmed  upregulation of ido1 via nc2  hybridization staining and quantitated  this particular response using image  analysis  the overall idea one up regulation  in this patient tumor at the site of the  io combination  is significant compared to all of the  other treatments in this patient's tumor  including another injection site that  contained  a combination of doxorubicin a more  classical chemotherapy  and the volumab and this comparison is  seen  when you are now comparing the two green  bars in this bar chart  so confirming the spatial profiling gene  expression results through  these orthogonal assays gives us a lot  of confidence  in this type of approach and this type  of high plex analysis  and so we were very pleased to see that  we were able to confirm the results  through our labs through these assays  so now just to wrap up here briefly um  you know we're excited about these two  technologies and the way that we can  combine them  for translational research we're really  using them to begin to understand and  gain insight into some of the early  mechanism of action of drugs  like checkpoint inhibitors as well as  the tumor microenvironment response  to these types of drugs when we  introduce them directly  into unique patient tumors  using the data like i just showed you we  can begin to uncover potential  actionable hypotheses for how to combine  drugs  not only for drug development purposes  but really in the future for precision  oncology purposes  so i'll end there uh thank you for  joining the presentation today and thank  you to everyone at nanastring in the  national stream tech access program  for their support in helping us generate  geomix data here early on  and of course a big thank you from the  presage team  to all of you for joining the talk today  oh excellent talk thank you so much  jason really appreciate it um if you  don't mind i have a few questions for  you  um first off yeah what is the typical  time point you evaluate  tumor response for the sibo micro dose  drugs  yeah that's a good question and that's  an important question  as it relates to the types of drugs that  we're studying so  typically in our clinical studies  we are able to remove or biopsy  tumors within an hour and as far out as  120 hours  following the intra-tumoral fevo  microdosing  and and largely this time point depends  on the mechanism of action  of the drug and the type of response  that we're looking to evaluate so for  example  we would select maybe a shorter time  point  for the evaluation if we're interested  in looking at early biomarkers  of resident immune cell activation  great another question here um can you  explain what you mean by phase  zero clinical studies yeah this is  probably a less familiar phase  in clinical and translational studies  phase  phase zero studies are clinical trials  that  have been developed in in response to  the exploratory  ind guidance put forth by the fda  and this guidance allows researchers  to administer sub-therapeutic microdose  quantities  of investigational new drugs and so  we've been able to leverage this  guidance  uh using our sivo uh platform  um in a unique way and that we can  deliver  this micro dose directly to the tumor  and achieve and achieve these spatially  resolved  higher concentrations of the drug  without systemically  exposing the patient to the to that drug  fantastic great overview and one final  question  how large are the spatially restricted  drug response zones in the tumor tissue  and how do you select your areas for  analysis such as placement of  aois or areas of interest for dsp  yeah so the typical drug response zone  or drug exposure zone within that  column of drug that we introduce into  patient tumors is about  anywhere from three sometimes four  millimeters in diameter  the co-injection of our sibo glow  microspheres which i  pointed out earlier in the talk they're  the small  foresting microspheres that stay  localized  at the sites of injection these really  enable  us to identify each one of those  drug exposure zones or injection sites  after we  cross section the tumor and after we  thin section  the fixed tumor for immunohistochemistry  and other assays like geomics  so by using these  fluorescent tracking microspheres it  allows us  to find each injection site and then  place areas of interest  like we do in the geomic system  and in some cases we can leverage  an adjacent tissue section that maybe  has a stain of particular interest for a  biomarker  that we can also leverage to uh place  areas of interest for uh types of  analyses like gl mix  all right great thank you so much and  with that i think we're at time for the  presentations but  i wish to thank both speakers again so  much for sharing their  insights with us and i hope everyone has  a good day
 

